   178	\section{Where the advantage lives: identity replacement}\label{sec:reset}
   179	% BEGIN-EV2
   180	\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget cut~\const{3} had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed, but after the headline contrast and the six fresh-market D2+H3 outcomes had been seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}). It exceeds one because the pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory $t$ interval across the six market means, [\site{rs_gd0_t_lo}, \site{rs_gd0_t_hi}], includes zero; the gap is positive on \site{rs_gd0_pos} of the six markets (\site{rs_gd0_pos_d} with the doubled searches), and most of its negative value comes from fresh5 (without fresh5, $S$ is \site{rs_s_loo5}). We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood. The seed intervals above hold the six markets fixed; leaving out one market at a time gives