 % BEGIN-EV2
 The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum beside it (Appendix~\ref{sec:app-design}) adds labels and disclosures. Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest} reports the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic. Tables~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, \ref{tab:ph-doubled}, \ref{tab:ph-q4}, \ref{tab:ph-welfare}, and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens} report exploratory analyses of existing records, and Tables~\ref{tab:ph-stability} and~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} the separate registered-configuration stability check; none of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals are those of Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2}. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (\site{r_ext_n} interval entries in the report's per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit contrast there is above rounding scale on only \site{r_ext_seeds} of \site{r_ext_total} seeds (the others are at most \site{r_ext_round} in absolute value), and \site{r_ext_cnt} of the \site{r_ext_tot} frozen resamples (\site{r_ext_share}) omit all three, so their standard error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs, list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the report has a bound beyond $\const{1000}$ in absolute value.
 
-\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. Two exploratory follow-ups after the report bear on this comparison. Continued searches of the \site{r_rb_cells} registered cells whose doubling check was flagged (up to three rounds each) all passed within three rounds; with their final winners substituted, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{r_rb_q1} under D2+H3 and B3 minus R3 is \site{r_rb_q4}, and the largest change of a market difference is \site{r_rb_max} (fresh3, where the difference becomes positive). The development runner-up comparator, C-thr(\const{12}), evaluated on the same six markets without churn, gives the attacker a pooled D2+H3 gain lower than against R3 (runner-up minus R3: \site{r_ra_est}, [\site{r_ra_lo}, \site{r_ra_hi}]; ``\site{r_ra_cat}''); under X7-SC with the doubled searches the sign is the opposite (\site{r_ra_x7d}). The registered direction holds for C$^*$, not for verified-history targeting in general. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. With each rule's strongest training-selected family the interval is [\site{r_env_lo}, \site{r_env_hi}], with \site{r_env_pos} of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the equivalence label is the most fragile part; beyond these six markets the sign is not established either, since the frozen interval contains zero and the searches share inputs; on these markets the registered direction tests reject zero for the prescribed searches, and another comparator would be a different comparison. The frozen D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner