-\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it stays near a third (\site{ch_fa_c5} at $p = \const{0.05}$) and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. On two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
