-For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and its measured advantage reverses: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5}. Against C$^*$, age-ranked audit priority holds its own only without churn; at the lowest tested rate the comparison is unresolved, and above it C$^*$ does better in the pooled estimate; against uniform audits its pooled advantage lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
