ts measured advantage reverses: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5} (\site{c_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches). Against C$^*$, the one verified-history comparator run under churn, age-ranked audit priority holds its own only without churn; at \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} the comparison is unresolved, and at \const{0.03} C$^*$ does better on every market; against uniform audits its pooled advantage lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
