+\paragraph{How firm the reversals are.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_5_neg} of the six markets and B3 minus R3 on \site{ch_q_5_neg} with the frozen searches, and on \site{ch_d_5_dneg} and \site{ch_q_5_dneg} with the doubled searches (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{ch_d_5_d}; B3 minus R3: \site{ch_q_5_d}). With the frozen searches, exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{ch_t_d5_lo}, \site{ch_t_d5_hi}] for C$^*$ minus R3 and [\site{ch_t_q5_lo}, \site{ch_t_q5_hi}] for B3 minus R3; they reach zero because the market values are very unequal, not because the sign varies. With the doubled searches fresh0 has the other sign for both contrasts, and the B3 minus R3 interval is [\site{ch_t_q5_dlo}, \site{ch_t_q5_dhi}]. The frozen B3 value at this rate depends on one flagged cell (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn}, doubled row). At $p = \const{0.03}$, B3 minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_q_3_neg} of six markets with the frozen searches and on \site{ch_q_3_dneg} with the doubled ones, so its positive pooled value there does not hold market by market. At that rate C$^*$ minus R3 is negative on \site{ch_d_3_neg} of six markets, but at $p = \const{0.02}$ only on \site{ch_d_2_neg}: the pooled reversal at that rate comes mostly from one market. With the frozen searches the $t$ intervals for C$^*$ minus R3 are [\site{ch_t_d2_lo}, \site{ch_t_d2_hi}] at \const{0.02} and [\site{ch_t_d3_lo}, \site{ch_t_d3_hi}] at \const{0.03}. At $p = \const{0.01}$ the C$^*$ minus R3 difference is \site{ch_d_1_x} with the frozen searches and \site{ch_d_1_d} with the doubled ones, so the comparison with C$^*$ is not resolved there, while the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{ch_q_1_x} and \site{ch_q_1_d}. \site{ch_inc} of the \site{ch_inc_n} doubling checks at $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05} and \site{ch_inc_mid} of the \site{ch_inc_mid_n} at \const{0.02} and \const{0.03} are flagged incomplete; the largest flagged change is \site{ch_flag_max}. Reselecting each rule's reference from its frozen menu under churn changes the reference in \site{ch_resel} of the \site{ch_resel_n} cells, by at most \site{ch_resel_max} in gain.
-\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it stays near a third (\site{ch_fa_c5} at $p = \const{0.05}$) and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. On two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. The decline is gradual, not a threshold, and it does not explain both crossings: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while B3 minus R3 stays positive until between \const{0.03} and \const{0.05}, because the gain also depends on the rest of the selected attack. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
+\paragraph{Where R3's audits go.} Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit} shows how each rule spends its audits under its selected attack. Under R3 the share of the attacker's jobs that is audited falls from \site{ch_fa_1} at $p = \const{0.01}$ to \site{ch_fa_2}, \site{ch_fa_3}, and \site{ch_fa_5}, while under C$^*$ it moves from \site{ch_fa_c1} to \site{ch_fa_c5} and under B3 it is \site{ch_fa_b5}. With the doubled searches R3's value at $p = \const{0.01}$ is \site{ch_fa_1d} (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}). At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{ch_yj_5} of the jobs and receive \site{ch_ya_5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{ch_est_5}. On two or three of the six markets, depending on the rate, R3's selected attack never replaces its identity (Table~\ref{tab:ph-churn-audit}); there the attacker does not hide among the newcomers but stays old, and, once past its own priority window, is audited only from what the priority phase and newest-first targeting leave. Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} does not cover such an attacker, since it bounds the audits of a replacement identity. Where the selected attack does replace (\site{ch_rep_n} markets at $p = \const{0.05}$), R3 audits between \site{ch_rep_lo} and \site{ch_rep_hi} of its jobs. The measured share falls at each of the four sampled rates; four rates cannot show whether the decline is smooth or has a threshold. Audit share alone does not determine gain: R3's audit rate on the attacker falls below B3's between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.02}, while the pooled B3 minus R3 gap stays positive through \const{0.03}. The diagnostics describe the setting; they do not isolate the cause, because churn also resets histories, ends bans, and changes the selected attacks. Consistent with them, the rise in R3's gain comes mainly from the attack's profit: in raw profit units it rises by \site{ch_pa_5} at $p = \const{0.05}$, while the reference's profit changes by \site{ch_pr_5} ([\site{ch_pr_5_lo}, \site{ch_pr_5_hi}]).
-\paragraph{Scope.} Only one verified-history comparator, C$^*$, was run under churn; the runner-up comparator of Section~\ref{sec:results} was evaluated without churn only, so this section does not show that verified-history targeting in general is robust to churn. Within this model, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ exists only while the attacker is almost the only source of new identities, and its advantage over uniform audits survives moderate churn but not the highest rate tested.
+\paragraph{Scope.} Only one verified-history comparator, C$^*$, was run under churn; the runner-up comparator of Section~\ref{sec:results} was evaluated without churn only, so this section does not show that verified-history targeting in general is robust to churn. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates, where the comparison is unresolved, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}.
-\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section~\ref{sec:results}, which holds the parameters and search budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
+\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities beyond the exploratory D2-family comparison of Section~\ref{sec:reset}, which holds the parameters and search budgets fixed and compares two separately searched winners. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
-\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn it falls and reverses (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the lowest rate. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
+\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. R3's registered advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities. With honest churn the pooled advantage over C$^*$ changes sign at the lowest rates and the pooled advantage over B3 at the highest (Section~\ref{sec:churn}); that follow-up is exploratory, uses illustrative rates and frozen references, runs one verified-history comparator, and leaves the comparison with C$^*$ unresolved at the lowest rate. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
-For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and its measured advantage reverses: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5}. Against verified-history targeting, age-ranked audit priority holds its own in this model only while the attacker is almost the only newcomer; against uniform audits it survives moderate churn but not the highest rate tested. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
+For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters and without honest churn, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets (under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on all six), and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. In an exploratory follow-up, most of the measured B3 minus R3 advantage under the prescribed searches disappears when attacker replacement is disabled: under the D2 family, neither pooled mean gap (B3 minus R3, or C$^*$ minus R3) is distinguishable from zero using exploratory market-level $t$ intervals. When honest providers also replace their identities, R3 spends its audits on honest newcomers, the share of the attacker's jobs it audits falls from \site{c_ch_fa1} to \site{c_ch_fa5} between $p = \const{0.01}$ and \const{0.05}, and its measured advantage reverses: at $p = \const{0.05}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{c_ch_d5} and B3 minus R3 is \site{c_ch_q5} (\site{c_ch_q5d} with the doubled searches). Against C$^*$, the one verified-history comparator run under churn, age-ranked audit priority holds its own only without churn; at \const{0.01} and \const{0.02} the comparison is unresolved, and at \const{0.03} C$^*$ does better on every market; against uniform audits its pooled advantage lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.

