             Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:
                 Registered Tests of Age-Ranked Audits
             and an Exploratory Limit Under Honest Churn

                                             Ömer Demirel

                                      Draft 10, November 2026

                                                 Abstract
         A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider
     can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery,
     costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that under
     uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement
     restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does
     not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity
     while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. Two registered tests,
     the second locked before six fresh markets were drawn from a Bitcoin block hash, compare it
     at matched budget and sanctions with uniform audits (B3) and a verified-history comparator,
     C∗ . Without honest churn, R3 lowers the attacker’s measured gain relative to B3 on every
     fresh market, though the registered replication passes on only 3 of six, and R3 and C∗ are
     “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within a pre-set margin of ±0.05 (C∗ minus R3: 0.021; 0.028
     after exploratory continued searches of the flagged cells; exploratory t interval over the six
     markets [−0.013, 0.054]); in an exploratory evaluation without churn, a second verified-history
     comparator is also equivalent to R3 within the margin, with a direction that depends on one
     market and on the search version (runner-up minus R3 under D2+H3: −0.010; R3 has the lower
     gain on 4 of six markets). Welfare attacks lower all three rules’ gross efficiency to about 0.840.
     Two exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report, locate R3’s advantage. When the attacker
     cannot replace its identity, most of the B3 minus R3 gap disappears (0.173 to −0.009). When
     honest providers also replace their identities with probability p per round, R3’s audits move to
     honest newcomers: the share of the attacker’s jobs that R3 audits falls from 0.366 at p = 0.01
     (0.505 with the doubled searches) to 0.042 at p = 0.05 (0.028), where C∗ minus R3 is −0.173 and
     B3 minus R3 is −0.052 (−0.033 with the doubled searches; the frozen value includes one failed
     search). The comparison with C∗ is not resolved at p = 0.01 or 0.02, where one market sets
     the pooled sign, and C∗ minus R3 is negative on all six markets at 0.03; the pooled B3 minus
     R3 gap stays positive through 0.03, though negative on three markets there, and is negative at
     0.05. At the tested rates, R3’s measured advantage over C∗ does not extend past the rates where
     the comparison is unresolved, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits does not reach the
     highest rate. 21 of the 72 churn doubling checks are flagged incomplete. The claims hold for
     the prescribed searches and frozen references, six markets, fixed parameters, illustrative churn
     rates, and one comparator under churn; C∗ was selected in development on gross efficiency in
     effect.


1    Introduction
A buyer of computation pays for work whose quality it cannot see. It can check a few jobs at a cost
and sanction a provider whose check fails. Exact checks of computation now exist, but they are

                                                     1
expensive and run after the response is served. FlashProve, for example, proves that a committed
integer model produced a given token under greedy decoding; the provider generates proofs after the
response is served, at about 70 to 95 times the cost of one forward pass [27, p. 18], for a window of
1024 tokens per request, and the client checks each proof on a CPU [27, pp. 16–17]. Its Lean proofs
cover the protocol; the equivalence of the executable prover and verifier to it is not proved [27, p. 17].
Such proofs detect a substituted computation; they do not prevent an incorrect response from being
served. If the checking party funds such checks, a budget forces a sample, and the questions become
which jobs to check and how to sanction a provider whose check fails.
    We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are
unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before
receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the
escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population
is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged
reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks [10]) are outside the model.

Checking newcomers is an old idea. Intensive verification until an identity has earned credi-
bility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29];
BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host’s jobs until the host
passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results [2, 1]; customs guidance recommends checking
all declarations of new importers for a period [35]. Checking can also depend on what a participant
reports [40]; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age
and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3’s per-
sistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything
else is held equal?

The rule. For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals
whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation,
immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to
identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary 1).
R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first L rounds, auditing all of them when
the round’s quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established
identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement
is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least min(s, ⌈r/2⌉) of the r residual audits,
s being its served jobs (Lemma 2 and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a
replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer
entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity’s first offer.

Two registered tests. Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped
before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments.
The first test (October 2026) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results
then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second
test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their
seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition,
stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test’s comparison.

Contributions. (i) A conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless under
uniform audits (Corollary 1), and a bound on the audits that age-ranked targeting gives a replace-


                                                    2
ment identity (Lemma 2). (ii) Two registered tests. Without honest churn, the prescribed searches
give R3 a lower measured attacker gain than uniform audits on every fresh market, and they classify
R3 and a development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent within the pre-set
materiality margin; R3’s priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other. (iii)
Two exploratory follow-ups, specified after the report, that locate R3’s advantage. It sits in the
attacker’s identity replacement: without replacement most of the gap to uniform audits disappears.
And it shrinks as honest providers replace their identities: R3 then spends its audits on honest
newcomers while an attacker that keeps its identity is rarely audited, and its measured advantage
over the comparator is unresolved at churn rates of 0.01 and 0.02 per round and gone at 0.03, where
it is negative on all six markets, while its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through 0.03
and is negative at 0.05. (iv) A record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments,
the failed predictions, and the follow-ups’ plans, to be released with the paper.

Results in brief. Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets and without honest churn, R3 and C∗
are “equivalent, direction R3 lower” within the registered margin under X7-SC and under D2+H3
(Section 8). Q4: the registered B3–R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on 3 of six fresh
markets, all three passes qualified by search flags. Q2 and Q3 (Appendix A): the priority phase and
newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects −0.083 and −0.095), and either
one alone accounts for most of the reduction; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3’s
gross efficiency to 0.840, and the registered W1 search (efficiency 0.997) supplies no efficiency bound
for randomised attacks. The two follow-ups are in Sections 9 and 10.


2    Related work
Verification-based probation. Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a
worker’s verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities [28, 29]; follow-on work treats
imperfect checks [36]. BOINC’s adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and
trusts a host above a threshold [2, 1]. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing
gold and peer evaluation [26]. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new
importers for a period [35], and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close
supervision of new firms [14]. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal long-
term contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the
track record [5]. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit
quota (Section 4).

Cheap pseudonyms. With cheap pseudonyms, unfavourable treatment of newcomers is the price
of cooperation [13]; whitewashing degrades peer-to-peer systems when turnover is high [11]; without
a central authority one attacker can create many identities [10]. Reputation mechanisms with pure
moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in [9]. Reputation without persistent
identity in markets of AI agents, with a one-time cash-out deviation, is studied in [15]; that model
has no verification budget.

Costly verification and audits. Costly state verification goes back to [31]; history-dependent
audit groups under restricted penalties go back to [18]; allocation with costly verification [3], lim-
ited punishments [20], financially constrained agents [21], and ex post verification with limited
penalties [24] assume persistent agents. Recursive inspection games spend a finite stock of in-
spections [34]; audit games optimise randomised inspection against a best-responding attacker [6];


